{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1333 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "cf0962e7b828a4ce416082bac36d94cd594b7dba9b8f90ccf0cd3507b367bc65" :edb/db-before-t 1332 :edb/db-after-t 1333 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534645 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:06:00.989Z" 13194139534645 true] [17592186054342 1100 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb N, \\quad \\mathbb Z, \\quad \\mathbb Q, \\quad \\mathbb R, \\quad \\mathbb C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $3.25$ belong?" 13194139534645 true] [17592186054342 1100 "Consider the following sets:\n$\\mathbb N, \\quad \\mathbb Z, \\quad \\mathbb Q, \\quad \\mathbb R, \\quad \\mathbb C$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $3.25$ belong?" 13194139534645 false] [17592186054342 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/assets/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following: \n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Z}$ nor $\\mathbb{N},$  so we can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\notin\\mathbb N, \\qquad 3.25\\notin\\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- However, notice that $3.25$ can be written as the ratio of two integers as follows:\n\n$$\n3.25=\\dfrac{325}{100}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{Q}.\n$$\n\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb{C},$ and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad 3.25 \\in \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbb Q, \\mathbb R,$ and $\\mathbb C$ only.\"" 13194139534645 true] [17592186054342 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/47/Source/Images/0d72f30929eeba5e9f3763f164935f47.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers**.\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n- $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Z}$ nor $\\mathbb{N}$, so we can write\n$3.25\\notin\\mathbb N, \\qquad 3.25\\notin\\mathbb Z$.\n- However, notice that $3.25$ can be written as the ratio of two integers as follows:\n$3.25=\\dfrac{325}{100}$\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n$3.25 \\in \\mathbb{Q}$.\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb{C}$, and we can write\n$3.25 \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad 3.25 \\in \\mathbb{C}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbb Q, \\mathbb R$, and $\\mathbb C$ only.\"" 13194139534645 false] [17592186054343 1100 "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-$?\n\n1. $-\\sqrt{3}$\n2. $-0.2$\n3. $0$\n4. $-\\sqrt{4}$" 13194139534645 false] [17592186054343 1100 "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-?$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$\n\n- $- 0.2$\n\n- $0$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}$" 13194139534645 true] [17592186054343 1145 "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative but not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $-0.2$ is negative, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n-0.2 = -\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-0.2$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $0$ is a rational number but not negative, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4} =- 2$, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 4 = -2 = -\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\sqrt 4$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139534645 true] [17592186054343 1145 "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative but not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $-0.2$ is negative, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n$-0.2 = -\\dfrac{1}{5}$\nTherefore, $-0.2$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- $0$ is a rational number but not negative, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- $-\\sqrt{4} =-2$, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n$-\\sqrt 4 = -2 = -\\dfrac{2}{1}$\nTherefore, $-\\sqrt 4$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139534645 false] [17592186054344 1100 "To which of the following sets do all the negative integers belong?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{Z}$\n2. $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$\n3. $\\mathbb{Q}^-$\n4. $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$" 13194139534645 false] [17592186054344 1100 "To which of the following sets do all the negative integers belong? \n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}^-$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$" 13194139534645 true] [17592186054344 1145 "The negative integers are $-1, -2, -3, \\ldots$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ contains all the integers, including the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ contains the positive integers and $0.$ So, it does not contain the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative rational numbers. Since all integers are rational numbers, $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative real numbers and $0.$ Since all integers are real numbers, $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" 13194139534645 true] [17592186054344 1145 "The negative integers are $-1, -2, -3, \\ldots$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ contains all the integers, including the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ contains the positive integers and $0$. So, it does not contain the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative rational numbers. Since all integers are rational numbers, $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative real numbers and $0$. Since all integers are real numbers, $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\"" 13194139534645 false] [17592186054727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301033 13194139534645 true] [17592186054727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301088 13194139534645 true] [17592186054727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301095 13194139534645 true] [17592186054728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301006 13194139534645 true] [17592186054728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301013 13194139534645 true] [17592186054728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301068 13194139534645 true] [17592186054728 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301081 13194139534645 true] [17592186054729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301020 13194139534645 true] [17592186054729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301040 13194139534645 true] [17592186054729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301047 13194139534645 true] [17592186054729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301054 13194139534645 true] [17592186054729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186301061 13194139534645 true] [17592186054986 1017 #edb/ref 17592186051839 13194139534645 false] [17592186054986 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301027 13194139534645 true] [17592186054986 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534645 true] [17592186054986 1100 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-1}$ belong?" 13194139534645 true] [17592186054986 1100 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$N, Z, Q, R, C$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $\\sqrt{-1}$ belong?" 13194139534645 false] [17592186054986 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/assets/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- First, we note that $\\sqrt{-1}=\\text{i}$ is a complex number. Therefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-1}\\in C.\n$$\n\n- Since the imaginary part of $\\sqrt{-1}$ is nonzero, it does not belong to $N,Z,Q,$ or $R.$ We can write\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-1}\\notin N,\\,\\sqrt{-1}\\notin Z,\\,\\sqrt{-1}\\notin Q,\\,\\sqrt{-1}\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" 13194139534645 true] [17592186055409 1017 #edb/ref 17592186054126 13194139534645 false] [17592186055409 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301075 13194139534645 true] [17592186055409 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534645 true] [17592186055409 1100 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nN,\\,Z,\\,Q,\\,R,\\,C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-5$ belong?" 13194139534645 true] [17592186055409 1100 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$N, Z, Q, R, C$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $-5$ belong?" 13194139534645 false] [17592186055409 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/assets/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $-5$ is negative, so it does *not* belong to $N,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-5\\notin N.\n$$\n\n- However, $-5$ is an integer, so it does belong to $Z,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-5\\in Z.\n$$\n\n- Every integer is a rational, real, and complex number. Consequently, $-5$ belongs to $Q,R,$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-5\\in Q,\\,-5\\in R,\\,-5\\in C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Z,Q,R,$ and $C.$\"" 13194139534645 true] [17592186055412 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534645 true] [17592186055412 1100 "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${R}_{0}^{-}?$\n\n- $-\\pi $\n- $\\sqrt{3}$\n- $-\\frac{4}{5}$\n- $0$" 13194139534645 true] [17592186055412 1100 "Which of the numbers below belong to $R_{0}^{-}$?\n\n1. $- π$\n2. $\\sqrt{3}$\n3. $-\\frac{4}{5}$\n4. $0$" 13194139534645 false] [17592186055412 1145 "Remember that ${R}_{0}^{-}$ is the set of all negative real numbers and $0.$\n\n- $-\\pi $ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $\\sqrt{3}$ is a positive number, so it does *not* belong to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\frac{4}{5}$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${R}_{0}^{-}.\\,✓$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." 13194139534645 true] [17592186301006 1017 #edb/ref 17592186301007 13194139534645 true] [17592186301006 1089 "q-104694" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301006 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534645 true] [17592186301006 1100 "Which of the numbers below belong to ${Q}^{-}?$\n\n- $47$\n- $-\\sqrt{12}$\n- $\\sqrt{10}$\n- $0$" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301006 1113 #uuid "161da8d4-ccfa-570a-837c-943ddb8b2d13" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301006 1145 "Remember that ${Q}^{-}$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $47$ is positive, not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\sqrt{12}=-2\\sqrt{3}$ is not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $\\sqrt{10}$ is positive, not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $0$ is not negative, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}^{-}.\\,\\times $\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None of those listed\"." 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301008 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301009 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301010 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301011 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1000 #edb/ref 17592186301012 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1041 "selection" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1104 #edb/ref 17592186301011 13194139534645 true] [17592186301007 1110 #uuid "009034eb-4dfc-5afb-9f77-266c35d15fd7" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301008 1034 "III and IV only" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301008 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534645 true] [17592186301008 1111 #uuid "def6a70d-48be-5761-80f6-ba7b752831b9" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301009 1034 "I, II and IV only" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534645 true] [17592186301009 1111 #uuid "4d69d869-e49d-5686-b3a3-4d06825320e9" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301010 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534645 true] [17592186301010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534645 true] [17592186301010 1111 #uuid "21fd1b83-20db-52b3-b526-75c6c68639c9" 13194139534645 true] [